# Swift Code Style — Swift 6

Follow Apple's Swift API Design Guidelines and Swift 6 strict concurrency.

## Language & concurrency

- **Swift 6 language mode** with strict concurrency checking (`-strict-concurrency=complete`).
- Use `async`/`await` and structured concurrency (`Task`, `TaskGroup`, `async let`). No
  semaphores or manual thread hopping; no `DispatchQueue` for new code unless bridging.
- `@MainActor`-isolate anything that touches UI state. Prefer module-wide
  `defaultIsolation = MainActor` (Swift 6.2) and opt out with `nonisolated` / `@concurrent`
  for CPU-bound work.
- Cross-actor types must be `Sendable`. Make value types `Sendable` by design; annotate
  reference types explicitly and justify any `@unchecked Sendable`.
- Never block the main thread on `await` over network/disk.

## Naming & API design

- Types `UpperCamelCase`; members, vars, funcs `lowerCamelCase`. No Hungarian/`m_` prefixes.
- Methods read as phrases at the call site; name by role, not type.
- Booleans read as assertions (`isEnabled`, `hasChanges`).
- Prefer `struct` and `enum` (value semantics) over `class`. Use `final class` when a
  reference type is genuinely needed.

## Error handling

- Model expected failures with typed `throws` (Swift 6 typed throws) or `Result`; reserve
  `fatalError`/`preconditionFailure` for programmer errors only.
- No force-unwrap (`!`) or `try!` in shipping code paths — use `guard let` / `if let`.
  (`!` is acceptable only in tests and `@IBOutlet`-style guaranteed setup.)

## Code organization

- One primary type per file; extensions for protocol conformances.
- Organize by **feature/domain**, not by technical layer-then-screen.
- `let` over `var`; explicit `private`/`internal` access control; smallest scope that works.
- Comments in **English**, explaining non-obvious intent only — not narrating the code.

## Multiplatform

- Gate platform code with `#if os(iOS)` / `os(macOS)` / `os(watchOS)` / `os(visionOS)`.
- Keep shared logic platform-agnostic; isolate platform specifics behind protocols.

## Dependencies

- **Swift Package Manager only.** Declare in `Package.swift` or the Xcode package list.
  Pin with `.upToNextMajor`. No CocoaPods, no Carthage. See the `swift-package` skill.
